WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:04.840
Movies are meant to inspire us, scare us,
make us laugh, and make us cry.

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:08.430
But sometimes they just leave us absolutely,
totally confused.

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:13.309
2017 was a particularly dense year of confounding
movies, so get ready to get confused.

00:00:13.309 --> 00:00:16.049
Here are a few films that will bust your brain.

00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:19.580
And consider yourself warned: there will be
spoilers.

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:21.400
The Bad Batch

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:25.440
Set in the near future, The Bad Batch is about
a group of criminal exiles who've been left

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.779
to live in a lawless, cannibal-riddled wilderness.

00:00:27.779 --> 00:00:32.710
The neon-drenched backdrop of Comfort — the
central society where humans eat rabbits and

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:37.480
not each other — serves as a stark contrast
to the mania going on beyond the fence, where

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:39.940
civilization and civility are null and void.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:44.960
In a grim introduction, Suki Waterhouse's
Arlen is tattooed with a prison number and

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:46.200
dumped in the desert.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:50.620
Soon after, she's abducted and drugged; and
her limbs are amputated and devoured by a

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:51.720
group of cannibals.

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:56.000
Months later, she stumbles across a mother
and daughter scavenging the landscape, and,

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.820
upon realizing they're cannibals, she takes
out the mother and abducts the daughter as

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:00.969
an act of revenge.

00:01:00.969 --> 00:01:05.229
The women turn out to be the family of Miami
Man, who sets out to search for them and makes

00:01:05.229 --> 00:01:08.109
an unlikely acquaintanceship with Arlen along
the way.

00:01:08.109 --> 00:01:12.289
He doesn't know she's responsible for his
daughter's disappearance, of course, so he

00:01:12.289 --> 00:01:15.770
asks her to help with the search, and the
pair strike a peculiar friendship.

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:19.979
Jason Momoa's character doesn't bother to
hide his brutality, and Arlen makes it known

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:22.889
that she hates his kind, and somehow it just
works.

00:01:22.889 --> 00:01:26.130
But this is where the film's plot starts to
get lazy and go off the rails.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:31.090
Arlen does help Miami Man find his daughter,
and when they're all finally reunited, somehow,

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:34.710
it's never revealed that Arlen put mommy dearest
in an early grave.

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:39.450
Instead, she rejects a chance to stay in Comfort
and shack up with Keanu Reeves' leader character,

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:43.659
and ventures back into the bitter beyond with
Miami Man, essentially taking the place of

00:01:43.659 --> 00:01:44.749
the woman she axed.

00:01:44.749 --> 00:01:50.689
By the end, the movie makes so little sense
that its title couldn't possibly be more appropriate.

00:01:50.689 --> 00:01:52.289
Personal Shopper

00:01:52.289 --> 00:01:56.139
Featuring apparitions, half-open doors, and
things-that-go-bump-in-the-night, Personal

00:01:56.139 --> 00:01:59.459
Shopper has all the trimmings of a traditional
supernatural horror.

00:01:59.459 --> 00:02:03.770
But Olivier Assayas' thoughtful meditation
on grief is anything but ordinary, swapping

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:06.840
jump scares for ambiguous, psychological trickery.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:11.510
The film centers on Kristen Stewart's Maureen,
a personal shopper by day and spirit medium

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:12.510
by night.

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:16.510
After her twin brother passes away from a
heart condition, Maureen sets down a paranormal

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:20.670
path of discovery to make good on a deal the
pair had during his lifetime: the first to

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:23.250
go would contact the other from beyond the
grave.

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:27.640
Throughout the film, Assayas crafts a nuanced
story that interweaves the narrative of Maureen's

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:30.890
grief with the gruesome slaying of her celebrity
boss.

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:35.269
This is where the lines between reality, the
paranormal, and her own wild imagination begin

00:02:35.269 --> 00:02:36.269
to blur.

00:02:36.269 --> 00:02:40.200
Whether Maureen really is communicating with
her late brother or being manipulated due

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:45.159
to her fragile mental state, is far from clear,
leaving a lot of room for interpretation and

00:02:45.159 --> 00:02:46.159
puzzlement.

00:02:46.159 --> 00:02:49.959
Assayas himself later clarified the meaning
of the movie -- that Maureen was simply grieving

00:02:49.959 --> 00:02:54.060
her loss the only way she knew how -- but
anyone who saw this one in the theaters last

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:58.900
year was almost certainly scratching their
heads after the credits rolled.

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:00.030
Colossal

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:02.859
This is a monster movie with a colossal twist.

00:03:02.859 --> 00:03:07.090
After repeated drunken misbehaviour, Anne
Hathaway's Gloria is thrown out of her boyfriend's

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:08.420
apartment in New York.

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:12.200
Determined to get her life on track, she returns
to her hometown in New England, where she

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:17.749
bumps into an old friend in Jason Sudeikis'
Oscar, and her drinking problems persist.

00:03:17.749 --> 00:03:22.489
At the same time, director Nacho Vigalondo
throws up surprise after surprise, starting

00:03:22.489 --> 00:03:26.569
with the casual introduction of a monster
attack in Seoul, South Korea.

00:03:26.569 --> 00:03:30.639
That may seem at odds with the film's palette,
but the attack does have a direct link with

00:03:30.639 --> 00:03:34.939
Gloria's story — as she's somehow inexplicably
linked to the beast.

00:03:34.939 --> 00:03:39.670
When Oscar discovers he also has the same
monster-replicating superpower, Colossal follows

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:41.370
another tonal shift.

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:46.629
Oscar shows his sinister side, callously manipulating
Gloria by threatening to use his own monster

00:03:46.629 --> 00:03:50.299
to kill thousands of people if she doesn't
comply with his demands.

00:03:50.299 --> 00:03:54.790
Oscar's chilling behavior turns the monster
storyline into a conduit for emotional and

00:03:54.790 --> 00:03:59.480
physical abuse and leaves Gloria with the
challenge of overcoming her demons, both real

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:00.519
and imagined.

00:04:00.519 --> 00:04:05.920
Vigalondo uses monster-movie tropes to provide
a shrewd commentary on very real issues, and

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:12.310
while it is executed well, it's bound to give
audiences whiplash with so many bizarre swerves.

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:13.859
Song to Song

00:04:13.859 --> 00:04:18.789
Terrence Malick's filmmaking style frequently
divides opinion, and Song to Song is no different.

00:04:18.789 --> 00:04:23.370
Malick depicts a love-hexagon of the privileged
in a way only he can, weaving a complex web

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:28.630
of infidelity, betrayal, broken hearts, and
sentimentality in fluid edits and unconventional

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:29.630
framing.

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:31.180
The narrative itself is straightforward.

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:35.730
Set in the cutthroat music industry, Rooney
Mara's hopeful musician Faye begins an ill-conceived

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:40.300
relationship with Michael Fassbender's powerful
music producer Cook to help her career.

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:45.060
However, she falls in love with Ryan Gosling's
B.V., another musician working closely with

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:47.650
Cook and begins a relationship with him as
well.

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:52.380
After forming a three-way friendship, Faye,
B.V., and Cook navigate the landscape of music

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:56.389
festivals and cocktail parties, as Faye's
infidelity continues.

00:04:56.389 --> 00:05:00.979
What makes Song to Song both brilliant and
baffling is its replication of memory and

00:05:00.979 --> 00:05:01.979
consciousness.

00:05:01.979 --> 00:05:05.669
The story unfolds as if Malick cherry-picked
individual characters' reflections directly

00:05:05.669 --> 00:05:09.650
from their brains and scrambled them together
into a collective stream of consciousness

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:12.120
that warps the linear narrative structure.

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:16.610
Like raw memories themselves, the flow of
the story abruptly diverts, changing direction

00:05:16.610 --> 00:05:22.050
on a whim, and by the end of it, it's hard
to figure out what just played out on-screen.

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:24.190
A Cure for Wellness

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:29.160
Set in the breathtaking backdrop of the Swiss
Alps, no one can claim Gore Verbinski's aesthetically

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:30.680
pleasing thriller lacks ambition.

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:34.770
In fact, A Cure for Wellness suffers from
being overly ambitious.

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:39.789
Like its mountainous terrain, the story twists,
turns, and ultimately disorients its audience.

00:05:39.789 --> 00:05:41.520
Events initially appear straightforward.

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:46.050
Dane DeHaan's up-and-coming executive Lockhart
is tasked with retrieving his company's CEO

00:05:46.050 --> 00:05:48.340
from a hiatus at a Swiss wellness center.

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:51.970
To his frustration, the staff at the center
won't allow him to talk to the guy, and on

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:54.599
his way out of the center, he's involved in
a car crash.

00:05:54.599 --> 00:05:58.970
He awakens from the accident to find himself
a patient at the same wellness center, with

00:05:58.970 --> 00:06:01.169
his leg broken and covered in plaster.

00:06:01.169 --> 00:06:05.699
It's then that Lockhart and his crutches squeak
their way through a hallucinogenic journey

00:06:05.699 --> 00:06:07.090
filled with freaky imagery.

00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:11.370
The center is hiding a terrible secret, and
Lockhart is on a mission to find out what.

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:15.819
The slow-reveal of the truth is frustrating
and borderline nonsensical, as we discover

00:06:15.819 --> 00:06:21.000
the center's doctor has been extending his
own age by siphoning off substances from patients.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:22.990
"No one ever leaves."

00:06:22.990 --> 00:06:27.440
By the time the film serves up its final surprise,
though, the audience's appetite for weirdness

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:30.120
has long been satisfied.

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:31.419
Raw

00:06:31.419 --> 00:06:36.669
Julia Ducournau brings a fresh look at eating
human flesh with Raw, the second cannibal-themed

00:06:36.669 --> 00:06:38.310
movie on this list.

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:42.960
Unlike The Bad Batch, Raw gets under the skin
because of its eerie authenticity, replacing

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:46.599
a dystopian wasteland with the familiar backdrop
of a university.

00:06:46.599 --> 00:06:52.080
This realistic depiction makes the French-Belgian
horror too strong for some; at the 2016 Toronto

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:55.110
Film Festival, there were reports of audience
members fainting.

00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:57.319
Such a visceral reaction is understandable.

00:06:57.319 --> 00:07:01.139
Raw begins like an innocuous coming-of-age
tale with a young woman leaving home to join

00:07:01.139 --> 00:07:04.080
veterinary school, where her sister is also
studying.

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:08.430
During an unforgiving hazing process, she
breaks her strict vegetarianism when she is

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:13.349
forced by her sister to consume a raw rabbit
kidney, and this is where things get weird.

00:07:13.349 --> 00:07:14.460
Really weird.

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:19.139
Having had a taste, our heroine then embarks
on a cannibalistic quest for flesh that quickly

00:07:19.139 --> 00:07:20.270
grows out of hand.

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:23.690
Ducournau doesn't broach such a taboo subject
gratuitously.

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:28.180
Raw is hard to comprehend, but the conclusion
will leave chills down your spine — and

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:30.720
a bite mark on the memory.

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:31.720
mother!

00:07:32.380 --> 00:07:36.520
Director Darren Aronofsky said the script
for mother! poured out of him like a "fever

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:41.699
dream", and the result dives to surprisingly
dark depths of nightmarish vulgarity, ultra-violence

00:07:41.699 --> 00:07:43.410
and barbaric bewilderment.

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:48.080
It begins with Jennifer Lawrence's title character
and Javier Bardem's 'Him' living a life of

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:53.509
quiet solitude, when Ed Harris' 'Man' makes
an unsolicited appearance at their door, asking

00:07:53.509 --> 00:07:56.879
to stay, followed by his wife, Michelle Pfeiffer's
'Woman'.

00:07:56.879 --> 00:08:01.449
Much like the flames that engulf the house
at the beginning and end, events rapidly blaze

00:08:01.449 --> 00:08:04.479
out of control as more and more people arrive.

00:08:04.479 --> 00:08:08.909
Its entirety takes place within the claustrophobic
and confined setting, which eventually descends

00:08:08.909 --> 00:08:12.289
into a war zone, and an all-out assault on
the senses.

00:08:12.289 --> 00:08:17.469
On top of graphic violence, mother's most
unnerving moments are delivered with everyday,

00:08:17.469 --> 00:08:22.590
social non-conforming malice, where strangers
show complete disregard for her wishes.

00:08:22.590 --> 00:08:26.789
Trying to decipher the surreality at face
value left many moviegoers guessing, which

00:08:26.789 --> 00:08:32.050
is why Aronofsky had to come out and tell
people he meant this to be a biblical allegory.

00:08:32.050 --> 00:08:36.750
Mother represents creation, Him represents
God, and the people are the destroyers of

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:37.750
both.

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:41.080
Considering how batty the movie is, though,
it could mean a whole bunch of other things

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:43.770
or nothing at all, at the same time.

00:08:43.770 --> 00:08:45.560
Killing of a Sacred Deer

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:48.350
Yorgos Lanthimos is no stranger to eccentricity.

00:08:48.350 --> 00:08:53.470
He directed 2016's The Lobster, a story about
a hotel where its single residents have 45

00:08:53.470 --> 00:08:57.370
days to find a new romantic partner or else
they turn into animals.

00:08:57.370 --> 00:09:00.700
Initially, The Killing of a Sacred Deer appears
less bizarre.

00:09:00.700 --> 00:09:04.860
Scratch a scalpel under the surface though,
and Lanthimos outshines his earlier effort.

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:09.660
The story focuses on Colin Farrell's talented
surgeon Steven who befriends a boy named Martin.

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:13.850
Sprinkled with the director's trademark deadpan
dialogue, there are numerous quirks and dark

00:09:13.850 --> 00:09:16.450
humor moments that require close inspection.

00:09:16.450 --> 00:09:20.130
There's the weird obsession with body hair,
and Nicole Kidman's character pretending to

00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:22.520
be an anesthetized patient just to be kinky.

00:09:22.520 --> 00:09:25.070
And that's nothing compared to story's core.

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:29.020
Martin is out for revenge, believing Farrell's
character to be responsible for the demise

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:30.090
of his father.

00:09:30.090 --> 00:09:33.940
Martin tells Steven he has placed a curse
on his family in an act of vengeance.

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:38.920
To undo the curse, Steven has to kill a member
of his own family, or they will all die slow

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:40.410
painful deaths.

00:09:40.410 --> 00:09:45.220
Much to Steven's exasperation, Martin's curse
begins to manifest as his children lose the

00:09:45.220 --> 00:09:46.420
ability to walk.

00:09:46.420 --> 00:09:48.520
"My legs, they're numb."

00:09:48.910 --> 00:09:53.300
How Martin enacts the curse is anyone's guess;
he's seemingly able to make symptoms come

00:09:53.310 --> 00:09:54.310
and go.

00:09:54.310 --> 00:09:57.850
Those looking for answers won't get anything
concrete either, and it's compounded by an

00:09:57.850 --> 00:10:01.020
ambiguous ending that gives little away.

00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:02.480
The Snowman

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:06.400
Expectations were high for this film because
director Tomas Alfredson was the mastermind

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:10.200
behind one of the finest horror films of a
generation, Let the Right One In, and The

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:12.710
Snowman boasted Michael Fassbender in the
lead.

00:10:12.710 --> 00:10:17.470
With such talent on both sides of the camera,
how did the film end up with a 7% score on

00:10:17.470 --> 00:10:18.580
Rotten Tomatoes?

00:10:18.580 --> 00:10:21.630
The issue certainly wasn't Jo Nesbø's source
material.

00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:26.330
His novel of the same name is the seventh
featuring alcoholic detective Harry Hole.

00:10:26.330 --> 00:10:30.320
Following the same premise as Nesbø's novel,
the story follows Hole as he searches for

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:34.830
a depraved serial slayer whose trademark is
to leave a snowman at his victim's houses.

00:10:34.830 --> 00:10:38.910
Unfortunately, issues with production melted
any hopes of The Snowman being a hit.

00:10:38.910 --> 00:10:43.170
Alfredson admitted that a tight production
schedule left the film as a "big jigsaw puzzle"

00:10:43.170 --> 00:10:44.570
with "a few pieces missing."

00:10:44.570 --> 00:10:49.700
The result is a disjointed and confusing experience,
with choppy editing, important storylines

00:10:49.700 --> 00:10:54.640
that are never concluded, and characters introduced
and later disappearing with no rhyme or reason.

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:59.790
Making matters worse, the marketing campaign
for the film became an unintentionally hilarious

00:10:59.790 --> 00:11:03.760
meme, which is hardly the way to make audiences
take your thriller pic seriously.

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:05.550
Thanks for watching!

00:11:05.550 --> 00:11:07.990
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:11:07.990 --> 00:11:10.020
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

